WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.340
It was on July 2nd, 1776, that the
Continental Congress voted to separate

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.509
the 13 colonies from British rule. Yet,
it's on July 4th that we celebrate

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:17.130
Independence Day. Why? Because it was on
that day that the Declaration of

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.869
Independence was adopted. Its audience?
Not just the citizens of the 13 states,

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:26.789
but the world at large, what the
Declaration refers to as "decent respect

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:30.990
to the opinions of mankind". It's on July
4th, then, that the revolutionary

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.020
principles spelled out in the
declaration first made their public

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.010
appearance. Here, for the first time in
history, a nation was resting its

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:42.059
legitimacy on the demands of human
nature and natural equality, and not on

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:46.890
the common blood, language, religion, or
traditions. That is why we celebrate

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.879
Independence Day on the 4th. It is the
principles set out in the Declaration

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:55.100
that define this new nation, not, as
Lincoln noted, "a mere change of masters".

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.030
July 4th is a day to celebrate America's
birth. It is also the day that Americans

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.690
gave birth to a set of ideas that
transformed not only this country but

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.440
eventually the world.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.780
Do you think the
Declaration of Independence was written

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.940
just for the United States, or for the
world at large? Let us know in your

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.360
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.799
in 60 seconds, and be sure to LIKE and
